{"ts": 1789777863.052462, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "`evaluate` is deterministic (repeats return identical values), so all variation across specs is real in-sample structure, not measurement noise. But in-sample structure \u2260 out-of-sample structure.\n\nLet me reason about the expected OOS shrinkage carefully:\n\n- **Sampling error**: SE of a Sharpe estimate at n=5000 is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22.\n- **Selection bias**: I evaluated ~70 specs and took the maximum. The top handful cluster tightly at 1.13\u20131.25 \u2014 a spread of ~0.12, well inside one SE. When many near-equivalent candidates are separated by less than the noise, the winner is disproportionately the one whose noise drew highest. Expected overstatement for the max of a correlated set this size is on the order of 0.2\u20130.3.\n- **Offsetting factor**: the core of this spec is not fragile. Features 34, 26, 37 each stand alone (0.83, 0.69, 0.64) with correct signs at high significance, and they're mutually uncorrelated \u2014 the jump from 0.83 to 1.25 is genuine diversification, which is the most reliably persistent source of Sharpe improvement. This is not a case of three noise features conspiring.\n\nSo I expect real OOS performance meaningfully above zero but clearly below 1.25. Centering around **0.95** reflects roughly 0.3 of shrinkage. I'll set sd = 0.25, covering both sampling error and the possibility that some in-sample edge is regime-specific and doesn't carry over.\n\nI'll choose {34, 26, 37} over {34, 26, \u221218}: they're statistically tied in-sample, but 37's standalone Sharpe (+0.644) rests on a positive-sign effect, and I slightly prefer it to 18 whose contribution enters via a sign flip."}
